A||||5||Mark||Rear Brake Drag||||5
Z||000000||Mark||10-03-1999||10:45 PM||||One of the rear brake calipers on my '86 GT doesn't seem to fully release (I feel alot of drag when the wheel is rotated by hand). The hand brake is not the problem, as I temporarily disconnected it.  Likewise, the caliper piston is not frozed, since I can push the piston back into the cylinder.  Can it be anything other than a "semi stuck" caliper piston?  Are these calipers rebuilable?     ||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||10-04-1999||01:33 AM||||The calipers are rebuildable, but unless you have experience rebuilding them, it's much easier to just buy a new or rebuilt caliper. Check the e-brake lever on the caliper and make sure it's releasing all the way. Sometimes they get rusty... shoot some WD-40 around the lever and work it back and forth with visegrips. I had a rear caliper stick on me once. The rotor got red hot and blew the rubber brake hose off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||10-04-1999||08:00 PM||||Check to see if the "sliders" are free. These are the bushings that the attachment bolts tighten up on. They ride in the caliper with O-rings at each end- when they stick it causes the outboard pad to drag.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Lynx||10-05-1999||04:41 AM||||I know on most cars you have to just rebuild them and they stop. But like batboy said this is NOT easy. Trust me i rebuilt the two rear calipers on my 85 because they leaked. I did it but it was a B**ch and yu need two people. Mainly hard because of the E-brake crap.||||reg||12
Z||000004||88 Fiero||10-05-1999||11:01 AM||||When you disconnected your rear brakes..and pull the calipers out...did you release the slider cable at the bottom rear of the car...reason is that one time I replace the rear brake pads on my '85 GT and forgot to loosen the slider cable...Once I got everything back together and test drive the car and came back to me garage...I smell burned toasted aroma in the rear and found out that the rotors are burning red...And will talking with my friend Keith...he mentioned if I ever loosened up that cable..in which I said no...so there it is..I learned my lesson...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||10-05-1999||05:49 PM||||I installed a rebuilt caliper on the side with excessive drag.  Now I've noticed that when the E-brake is released, the other caliper doesn't fully release (I have no problems with the service brake hanging up).  The cables appear to be nice and free, but to be sure, I'll disconnect it and see if the E-brake lever returns fully on its own.  I'm wondering if too much friction exists in the seal under the parking brake lever.  Any ideas on lubricating this seal, say with silicone grease? ||||reg||1
